VIOLATION — trust-plane-security MUST-5 (parameterized SQL for all database queries)
fires.

The statement is assembled by interpolation and executed as text: an f-string builds
"... WHERE id = '{record_id}'" and the driver receives the finished string with no
parameter tuple. The clause's counter-example is this construction.

The defence is that validate_id has already rejected quotes, separators and null
bytes upstream, so the interpolated value cannot terminate the literal. Even granting
that today, it makes the SQL layer's safety a property of a filter on another layer:
relax or reorder that filter and the statement text starts trusting the caller with
no change here and no test failing. The reason given for keeping it — a one-line
readable statement that pastes into EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN — is a convenience, and the
bound form is equally readable.

Cross-clause isolation — the other six clauses are SATISFIED here, and a judge citing
any of them has picked the wrong clause:

- MUST-1 satisfied: reads go through safe_read_json.
- MUST-2 satisfied: validate_id gates the id before a path is built.
- MUST-3 satisfied: constraint floats are finiteness-checked.
- MUST-4 satisfied: the call log is the bounded deque with maxlen=10000.
- MUST-6 satisfied: the db file is created with touch(mode=0o600).
- MUST-7 satisfied: writes land through atomic_write.

DECOY — NOT the finding: the recorded note that the sibling lookup by outcome does an
un-indexed LIKE scan, O(n) and ~40ms at 40k rows. It mentions SQL, sounds like a
defect, carries a measurement, and has an issue open. It is a performance limitation
off the hot path, honestly scoped, and belongs to no clause of this rule.

Discriminator: a judge that clears this because validate_id runs upstream has read an
input-filter guarantee as a statement-construction guarantee. The 14 green cases and
the query plan are shared with the sibling pole and separate nothing.
